		VERSIONING NOTE (per this project's convention): bump <version> above
		on every future edit to this file's Lua/XML behavior; the file name
		itself (Moen_Smart_Shower_Standalone_driver.xml/.lua) must NOT change.

		================================================================
		DECISION RECORD - v102 (Pool proxy) -> v200 (Thermostat + Extras)
		================================================================
		Full decision record: Phase0_Decision_Record.md (Moen Smart Shower
		UI Re-Engineering Plan project). Summary carried here per this
		project's convention of keeping architectural memory in the header:

		WHY v102 WAS WRONG (kept for history, see also the superseded note
		below): v102 modeled the shower as a Pool/Spa proxy. That correctly
		got the shower out of the light_v2 lighting-scene/voice group (a
		real hazard - see the v100->v102 note below), but it left the
		driver's primary interaction - firing a saved preset - completely
		absent from Navigator. Presets only existed as Composer
		actions/commands. The device also read as a pool heater, not a
		shower, and lived under Comfort -> Pools.

		WHY THERMOSTAT + EXTRAS (v200): Control4 has no dedicated shower
		proxy. Of the three architectures considered (Thermostat+Extras,
		Pool+aux-remap, Light_V2+Extras - the last rejected outright, see
		below), Thermostat+Extras was chosen because:
		  1. Extras is a first-class, documented feature of the Thermostat
		     proxy (has_extras, EXTRAS_SETUP_CHANGED, EXTRAS_STATE_CHANGED),
		     giving Navigator a native (non-HTML) on/off switch + one button
		     per real preset - the actual interaction the user wants.
		  2. Agent/Wakeup exposure - the risk that could have killed this
		     option outright - was checked LIVE against the real EA-1
		     project's Agents panel: this OS Management Package (3.4.3) has
		     NO Wakeup Agent at all, and Scheduler's events are individually
		     named/explicit with none referencing this device or any
		     thermostat/pool-classed device. Unlike Light V2 (genuinely
		     swept by "all lights on/off"), there is no house-wide automatic
		     sweep of Thermostat-classed devices - automation against one is
		     installer-configured per device via Composer Actions, not
		     implicit class enrollment. Confirmed safe.
		  3. A throwaway test driver (thermostatV2 proxy, has_extras=true,
		     zero Moen code) was iterated live against the real EA-1 until
		     structurally clean. The blocker every earlier attempt hit -
		     Composer's "WARNING! This device's C4i is badly formed" - was
		     traced to placing <capabilities>
    <navigator_display_option proxybindingid="5001">
      <display_icons>
        <Icon width="1024" height="1024">controller://driver/Moen_Smart_Shower_Standalone_driver_v200/icons/device/shower/experience_1024.png</Icon>
        <Icon width="512" height="512">controller://driver/Moen_Smart_Shower_Standalone_driver_v200/icons/device/shower/experience_512.png</Icon>
        <Icon width="300" height="300">controller://driver/Moen_Smart_Shower_Standalone_driver_v200/icons/device/shower/experience_300.png</Icon>
        <Icon width="90" height="90">controller://driver/Moen_Smart_Shower_Standalone_driver_v200/icons/device/shower/experience_90.png</Icon>
        <Icon width="70" height="70">controller://driver/Moen_Smart_Shower_Standalone_driver_v200/icons/device/shower/experience_70.png</Icon>
      </display_icons>
    </navigator_display_option>
 nested inside
		     <connection><classes><class> (matching how v102 structures its
		     Pool capabilities). The FIX, confirmed against this project's
		     own real working IntelliFire driver (also thermostatV2), is
		     that <capabilities> must be a TOP-LEVEL sibling of
		     <connections>/<config> - see below. Connection classname is
		     THERMOSTAT, not THERMOSTATV2.

		v201, FIRST REAL-DEVICE RESULT: v200 was applied to the actual Shower
		device (427) live. Current temperature displayed correctly (from
		TEMPERATURE_CHANGED). The Extras tab RENDERED (has_extras/capabilities
		were correct) but came through EMPTY - no switch, no preset buttons.
		Root cause: C4:SendToProxy's parameter table for EXTRAS_SETUP_CHANGED/
		EXTRAS_STATE_CHANGED used the UI-facing field name (extras_setup /
		extras_state) as the Lua table key. Per the DriverWorks Thermostat
		Notifications doc, the actual wire parameter for both is literally
		named "XML" - extras_setup/extras_state is only what it's called once
		it reaches the UI, not the SendToProxy key. v102's own pool code
		already used this exact convention for its XML notifications
		(POOL_HEATMODE_LIST_CHANGED / POOL_HEATMODE_CHANGED both send
		{XML=...}) - missed carrying it over the first time. Fixed in the
		.lua (see SyncExtras). Icons also replaced this version - see below.
		STILL TO CONFIRM after this fix: that the switch/buttons now actually
		appear and round-trip taps.

		v202, THREE MORE FIXES:
		1. PACKAGING MISTAKE (mine, not a driver bug): v200/v201 were
		   packaged as "Moen_Smart_Shower_Standalone_driver_v200.c4z" /
		   "..._v201.c4z" - a DIFFERENT outer package filename than the
		   original "Moen_Smart_Shower_Standalone_driver.c4z" that's bound
		   to the real device. Composer matches "Add or Update Driver" to an
		   already-bound device by that package filename (visible as
		   "Device File" on the Summary tab), not just the internal
		   driver.xml <name>. Loading a differently-named package creates a
		   SEPARATE catalog entry instead of updating the bound device in
		   place - which is why the icon change appeared to do nothing: the
		   bound device was still running the old package. v202 is
		   repackaged as EXACTLY "Moen_Smart_Shower_Standalone_driver.c4z",
		   matching the versioning rule at the top of this file (bump
		   <version>, never the file name) that I broke starting at v200.
		2. NO ON/OFF TOGGLE ON THE NATIVE THERMOSTAT CARD: capabilities had
		   <hvac_states> (which describes STATE feedback, e.g. "the system
		   is currently Heating") but not <hvac_modes> (the SELECTABLE mode
		   list Navigator actually uses to draw mode buttons) - confirmed as
		   two distinct capabilities on the Thermostat Capabilities doc
		   page. Added <hvac_modes>Off,Heat</hvac_modes> above. This should
		   put an Off/Heat toggle directly on the thermostat card, wired to
		   the same Action_ShowerOn/Off as the Extras switch.
		3. INBOUND COMMAND NAMES WERE WRONG WORD ORDER: found the actual
		   "Thermostat Unhandled Commands" reference page (a page this
		   project's earlier research missed - it lists precisely what a
		   protocol driver receives from the proxy, as opposed to the
		   Notifications page which only covers the other direction). The
		   real commands are SET_MODE_HVAC and SET_SETPOINT_HEAT - not
		   SET_HVAC_MODE / SET_HEAT_SETPOINT as previously inferred by
		   analogy. Fixed in the .lua RFP handler. Param key names (MODE,
		   SETPOINT) are still inferred by symmetry with the matching
		   outbound notification, since this reference page only lists
		   command names and one-line descriptions, not parameter tables -
		   if still wrong, the "else" branch logs the real strCommand.

		INBOUND COMMAND NAMES ARE AN INFERENCE, NOT DOC-CONFIRMED: the
		per-proxy Thermostat doc (snap-one.github.io/
		docs-driverworks-proxyprotocol-tstat) fully documents the outbound
		notifications this driver sends UP to the proxy (TEMPERATURE_CHANGED,
		HEAT_SETPOINT_CHANGED, HVAC_MODE_CHANGED, ALLOWED_HVAC_MODES_CHANGED,
		EXTRAS_SETUP_CHANGED, EXTRAS_STATE_CHANGED - all confirmed present on
		that page with parameter tables). It does NOT document the inbound
		commands the proxy sends DOWN to the driver when a user taps the
		native thermostat controls. SET_HVAC_MODE / SET_HEAT_SETPOINT are
		inferred by direct analogy with every other proxy already proven in
		this project (SET_LEVEL for light_v2, SET_POOL_HEATMODE /
		SET_POOL_SETPOINT / SET_AUX_MODE for pool - "SET_" + the matching
		_CHANGED notification's base name is the pattern every one of them
		follows). If wrong, the RFP dispatcher's "else" branch logs the real
		incoming strCommand instead of guessing blindly again - check the
		log during the Phase 1 acceptance test and fix the name in one pass
		if it doesn't match. This does not affect safety either way: an
		unrecognized command is a silent no-op, never a default action.

		NOTE ON PROXY CHOICE (v102 change, superseded above but kept for
		history): earlier versions modeled the shower and each outlet as
		light_v2 proxies. That put the shower in the "all lights on/off"
		scene/voice-command group, which could turn on water unexpectedly -
		a real hazard, not just cosmetic. v102 moved to Pool as a closer
		structural match than light_v2 while staying out of Lighting. v200
		keeps that same property (out of the lighting/voice-all group) via
		Thermostat instead.

		NOTE ON CONNECTIONS: there is deliberately NO <connection> block for
		a fixed network binding (unlike the bridge variant's TCP binding
		6001) - unchanged from v102. REST calls via C4:urlGet/urlPost don't
		need one. The Pusher WebSocket binding is allocated dynamically by
		websocket.lua itself at runtime, unchanged from v102 - see the .lua
		header for details. NONE OF THE TRANSPORT/PROTOCOL LAYER CHANGED IN
		THIS VERSION - only the proxy/UI surface. See the .lua file; REST
		auth, polling, and the Pusher subscription-wait fix are untouched.
	-->
